#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228860 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/id #uuid "d77c4a1b-863b-57ad-be77-44f2d802f554" :question/math-academy-id "e-21677" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2 - xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2)$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}((2 - xy^{2}) + (2 - x_{new}(y_{new})^{2})) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4 - xy^{2} - x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2 - 0.5xy^{2} - 0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 2 - 0.5xy^{2} - 0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2 = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= 0.5 \\cdot 2(y_{new})^{2} + y_{new} - 1 - 2 + 0.5 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1^{2} \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{2} + y_{new} - 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$\n\nWe'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 2(y_\\text{new}) + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 2.5} {2(y_\\text{new})_n + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1) = 1$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y_{new})_{1} &= 1 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 1.166667 \\\\ (y_{new})_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 1.158333 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{4} \\mid \\approx 1.158312\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1.16}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228861 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/id #uuid "9148676f-5899-539c-9b95-4a0d8d225056" :question/math-academy-id "e-21679" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2y^2 - \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x = 1$, the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n1. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - 1 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n2. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - \\dfrac12 (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n3. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n4. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 - \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2y^{2} - \\sqrt{x} + 2(y_{new})^{2} - \\sqrt{x_{new}}) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= y^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} + (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x_{new}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + y^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} + (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x_{new}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{2} - 1 - 1^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "527790c6-44ec-5f59-950d-f51dddb0ae56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228094}] :question/id #uuid "f3a732f7-f589-5b15-910f-f8c16bf26587" :question/math-academy-id "q-333426" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - y^{3}, y(0.5) = 1$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1)$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fc0d489-5529-5117-8fe3-8a38d816815d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = y_{new} - (x_{new})^{2}y_{new} - y - x^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228102} {:answer/id #uuid "a89c26d4-fe03-515d-a89e-0cca73fe1154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = y_{new} - (x_{new})^{2}\\sqrt{y_{new}} - y - x^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228103} {:answer/id #uuid "c656354b-59ca-5589-bc3f-19d667645658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = y_{new} - 2(x_{new})^{2}\\sqrt{y_{new}} - y - 2x^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228104} {:answer/id #uuid "30e06905-55c7-5e70-860b-0c64a42a9dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = y_{new} - (x_{new})^{2}\\sqrt{y_{new}} - y - (x_{new})^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228105} {:answer/id #uuid "7bad23ec-d566-5296-8598-7d3e0c126029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = y_{new} + (x_{new})^{2}\\sqrt{y_{new}} - y - x^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a89c26d4-fe03-515d-a89e-0cca73fe1154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = y_{new} - (x_{new})^{2}\\sqrt{y_{new}} - y - x^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228103} :answer-field/id #uuid "38d65971-3568-5348-a9a7-7d0179cca76e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228101}] :question/id #uuid "372050b3-acc3-51df-a046-2619f3775d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333430" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = 4x^{2}\\sqrt{y}, y(0) = 2$\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx = 0.5$, the new value $y_{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g(y_{new})$ in terms of the new values $x_{new}$ and $y_{new}$, and the current values $x$ and $y$?"}]}
